  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'A convex lens is a transparent optical lens that is thicker at the centre
    and thinner at the edges. It is also called a converging lens because it bends
    parallel rays of light inward so that they meet at the principal focus. The straight
    line passing through the optical centre and the two centres of curvature is called
    the principal axis.


    The image formed by a convex lens depends on the position
    of the object. When the object is beyond twice the focal length, the image is
    real, inverted, and diminished. At twice the focal length, the image is real,
    inverted, and the same size. Between the focal point and twice the focal length,
    the image is real, inverted, and magnified.


    When the object is placed between
    the focal point and the optical centre, the lens forms a virtual, upright, and
    magnified image on the same side as the object. Convex lenses are used in magnifying
    glasses, cameras, microscopes, telescopes, projectors, and spectacles for correcting
    long-sightedness.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
